claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 02:33:08 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> The current rule is that any message that hasn't received a reply from
>> the operator is considered in the inbox.
>>
>> That's a good start, but it means that a thread can never end - it
>> always sits in the inbox.
>
> Right, and it bites both ways. If you reply "applied, thanks", that
> lands in my inbox, and the only way I can clear it is to reply, which
> lands in yours. Under the reply rule, the last word always leaves
> someone with an open message.
>
> One constraint I'd keep: mail-tag.sh derives every tag from the list,
> so a rebuilt database gives the same answer. A closed thread should
> work the same way, so no notmuch-only tag like "muted" that is lost
> when the database is rebuilt.
>
>> What are some ways we can address this?
>
> Here are four, from the one I'd pick down to ones I'd avoid:
>
> 1. A close header. The message that ends a thread carries a header,
>    e.g. "X-Lab-Status: closed" (or "applied", "rejected", "answered").
>    mail-tag.sh then treats the whole thread as closed: no message in
>    it is tagged inbox, and the closing message itself never needs a
>    reply. Whoever has the last word sends one message, and the thread
>    is closed for everyone. It stays derived from the list. The
>    closing message can also explain the outcome, so the resolution
>    stays in the archive, which the "Post resolution to list" step in
>    zib/patch-review-workflow.org needs anyway.
>
>    I'd use a header and not a line in the body on purpose. A reply
>    quotes the body, so a "Status: closed" line in the body gets
>    quoted back and closes the thread when nobody meant to. That is
>    the sclr failure from CLAUDE.md. A header can't be quoted by
>    accident.

This seems like a pretty good idea. Here are some things I'm not sure
about though:

- How does it work when a thread has multiple levels to it? Say there's
  a first message, and then two separate replies to it. Then replies
  each of those replies - so essentially two branches. Does adding this
  header kill the entire thread, or just the branch?
- Is it possible to just add a header to an existing message? The files
  are in version control, so we could modify the files instead of
  posting a new message. Essentially we're adding metadata to existing
  files.



> 2. Close patches when trunk contains them. Patches already carry a
>    Change-Id that notmuch indexes. A patch whose change id is
>    reachable from trunk@upstream is accepted, and its thread can drop
>    out of the inbox with no message at all. That covers your common
>    case automatically. The catch is that tagging would then depend on
>    the repo as well as the list, and it only works if the change id
>    survives the way you apply patches. I haven't checked whether it
>    does. A rejected patch would still need an explicit close.

No I don't think we need this, at least not right now. I'd like to try
to keep things as simple as possible and derive the list state from the
mail branch.

> 3. A per-thread rule: a thread is open only while its newest message
>    is from someone else. That's tidier, but it doesn't solve your
>    problem. Whoever wrote last still leaves the other side open.

Rejected. It doesn't solve the problem at all. It's the current state
we're in.

> 4. Expiry, e.g. close a thread after N days without new mail. I'd
>    avoid it, because the tags would depend on when you index, and a
>    thread could close before anyone actually dealt with it.

Rejected. At some point we could set up scanners to identify stale
threads, but we don't want a rule to close them automatically.

>> A common case for me would be to accept a patch, and so there's no need
>> for a reply. Or I asked a research question, and got the info I need.
>
> My suggestion is 1 as the general mechanism and 2 on top of it for
> accepted patches. For a research answer you'd send a one-line close,
> something like "Got it, thanks" with the header set. Accepting a patch
> would need nothing from you.
>
>> I have a few ideas but I want to see what model comes up with.
>
> I'm curious which of yours overlap with these. If you settle on one,
> I'll send a patch to dotfiles/notmuch/hooks/mail-tag.sh and update the
> "Indexing mail" section of zib/patch-review-workflow.org to match.

Another idea I had was an archive dir, and so mute a thread by moving
messages into the archive dir. This can be done on a per-message basis,
and doesn't change any of the existing rules. i.e. if claude sends the
last message, and I archive it, it will no longer show up in my inbox
but claude doesn't see it as something as needing to be
responded. Archived messages would add the archived tag and remove inbox
tag.
